{"title":"Chebyshev and the history of the Prime Number Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>This page is a detailed description of Chebyshev's attempt to prove the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime number theorem\">prime number theorem</a>, and a bit of the history of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime number theorem\">prime number theorem</a> in general.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#History\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">History</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Chebyshev&#39;s_statement\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Chebyshev's statement</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"History\">History</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chebyshev_and_the_history_of_the_Prime_Number_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: History\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In 1850, Russian mathematician Pafnuty Lvovich Chebyshev made a major breakthrough by showing a statement that supports the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime number theorem\">prime number theorem</a>. It states:\n</p><p>\"Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/2/3e299f58fd2ed38d3720151e5404fb5599f0b040.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi (n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> denote the number of primes that is less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>There exist numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00322295674c54c5413520f7493470ff32ae3cec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d4142d351ef99cc26a94e44c1912d904dd4a0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/8/2981ef8d0893e758e7530a87064c12dbcfa95353.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[c_1 \\frac{x}{\\log{x}} \\le \\pi (x) \\le c_2 \\frac{x}{\\log{x}}\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"36\" /> \"\n</p><p>Additionally, he also showed that if the ratio of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/e/5fe74925ffdea4d77d24c11d49d49e5f86601103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi (x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/6/a860da362b851970416fef3d59d7f2cf12a2c80e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{\\log{x}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"43\" height=\"38\" /> goes toward a limit as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/0/7d0eb486837b6e2d5c5e6f6a075ea764200db0c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\to \\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"10\" />, then the limit is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Finally, in 1896, French mathematician Jacques Hadamard and Belgian mathematician Charles-Jean-Gustave-Nicholas de la Vallee-Poussin both (independently) produced a proof of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime number theorem\">prime number theorem</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Chebyshev.27s_statement\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Chebyshev's_statement\">Chebyshev's statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chebyshev_and_the_history_of_the_Prime_Number_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Chebyshev&#39;s statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/2/3e299f58fd2ed38d3720151e5404fb5599f0b040.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi (n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> denote the number of primes that is less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>There exist numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00322295674c54c5413520f7493470ff32ae3cec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d4142d351ef99cc26a94e44c1912d904dd4a0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/8/2981ef8d0893e758e7530a87064c12dbcfa95353.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[c_1 \\frac{x}{\\log{x}} \\le \\pi (x) \\le c_2 \\frac{x}{\\log{x}}\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"36\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chebyshev_and_the_history_of_the_Prime_Number_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be a prime and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be an integer. Since\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/1/b21413b7b343bdcee976ef9120a77dbbc64ac77a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dbinom{2n}{n} = \\frac{(2n)!}{(n!)(n!)}\\]\" width=\"131\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/3/7239867395424b0180bfb7d0156da6d583ea9eef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom{2n}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"50\" height=\"55\" /> exactly\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/e/f4ec44919343d3afe8298ee325f546608b9b9afa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{i=1}^{\\lfloor \\log_{p}{(2n)} \\rfloor} (\\lfloor \\frac{2n}{p^i} \\rfloor - 2 \\cdot \\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^i} \\rfloor)\\]\" width=\"193\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>times.\n</p><p>Therefore, since\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/9/9b9f3b35ca9dc529c3b8392561ba8d2444d7241b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\lfloor \\frac{2n}{p^i} \\rfloor - 2 \\cdot \\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^i} \\rfloor \\le 1\\]\" width=\"148\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p>, we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/e/f4ec44919343d3afe8298ee325f546608b9b9afa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{i=1}^{\\lfloor \\log_{p}{(2n)} \\rfloor} (\\lfloor \\frac{2n}{p^i} \\rfloor - 2 \\cdot \\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^i} \\rfloor)\\]\" width=\"193\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/c/74cba3cbc7aaa33a2ede37ba0c7cdc21c8943a67.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\sum_{i=1}^{\\lfloor \\log_{p}{(2n)} \\rfloor} 1\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/e/d2efca8c9f2285055c86802ae6ce5cd81d5c6366.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=\\lfloor \\log_{p}{(2n)} \\rfloor\\]\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>so if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/1/441b5c2e732fbfe11e2ef3de83121269fde0ecfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^r | \\dbinom{2n}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"72\" height=\"55\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b8c68ab789d59a7b5eaf6043a4897eb3421729.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^r \\le 2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Repeated applications of that gives\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/7/e17b331e6820dcffdacebc8c44fc980522ea55aa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dbinom{2n}{n} \\le (2n)^{\\pi (2n)}\\]\" width=\"134\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Additionally,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/4/9948d122ea8587dccc6b80113bccef96f90f6092.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^n=\\prod_{a=1}^n {2} \\le \\prod_{a=1}^n {\\frac{n+a}{a}} = \\frac{(n+1) \\cdot (n+2) \\cdot \\dots \\cdot 2n}{n!} = \\dbinom{2n}{n} \\le \\sum_{a=0}^{2n} {\\dbinom{2n}{a}} = 2^{2n}\\]\" width=\"618\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>so\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/6/f663718552340eeedeb80b6d8327f25d9df7573c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^n \\le (2n)^{\\pi (2n)}\\]\" width=\"108\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/f/8bf6743d0722053d54666823f771f80ae6267682.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies n \\cdot \\log {2} \\le \\pi (2n) \\cdot \\log {2n}\\]\" width=\"226\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/9/289c47313e57d576247891d9e924aa18b8b1789a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies \\pi (2n) \\ge \\frac{n \\cdot \\log {2}}{\\log {2n}}\\]\" width=\"170\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/a/56ac8faa1083716548a4e3882160508f33966b23.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies \\pi (n) \\ge \\frac{\\log{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{n}{\\log {n}}\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Now, we note also that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/9/1294e8e8b61621221493ed39ab03d20d16bc190f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\prod_{\\text{p is a prime from n to 2n}} {p} | \\dbinom{2n}{n}\\]\" width=\"208\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>so\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/b/78b6c4fabae1048fec42ee0c5ed3977ea229237b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n^{\\pi (2n) - \\pi (n)}=\\prod_{\\text{p is a prime from n to 2n}} n &lt; \\prod_{\\text{p is a prime from n to 2n}} p \\le \\dbinom{2n}{n} \\le 2^{2n}\\]\" width=\"571\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/f/13f577fee75b34f38f32243705e6e782c99fa64c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\pi (2n) - \\pi (n) \\le \\log {4} \\cdot \\frac{n}{\\log {n}}\\]\" width=\"216\" height=\"36\" />\n</p><p>We have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/8/f18bb14a22826594ca0322d31bd54009ffca3438.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\log {2n} \\cdot \\pi (2n)+\\log {n} \\cdot \\pi (n)= \\log {2} \\cdot \\pi (2n) + \\log {n} \\cdot (\\pi (2n) - \\pi (n)) \\le \\log {2} \\cdot \\pi (2n) + n \\cdot \\log {4} \\le 3n \\cdot \\log {2}\\]\" width=\"725\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/5/fa5f23047d55984405be865d582f6ff1b78056af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi (2n) \\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d8a348130d140aad5a9c23958578539f753241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> (because there is at least a half of even numbers).\n</p><p>Repeated addition of that gives\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7c24c67e18ebcee48668437fc4cc4badbcd82e7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\pi (2n) = \\frac{1}{\\log{2n}} \\cdot (\\log {2n} \\cdot \\pi (2n)- \\log {n} \\cdot \\pi (n))+(\\log {n} \\cdot \\pi (n)- \\log {\\frac{n}{2}} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{n}{2}))+ (\\log {\\frac{n}{2}} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{n}{2})- \\log {\\frac{n}{4}} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{n}{4}))+ \\dots\\]\" width=\"785\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/7/857c10b2e34e8489eac4cefff51d79659cb2dd63.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\frac{1}{\\log {2n}} \\cdot (3n \\cdot \\log {2}+\\frac{3n}{2} \\cdot \\log {2}+\\frac{3n}{4} \\cdot \\log {2}+ \\dots)\\]\" width=\"399\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/4/c3433f3ba6ccc7ab639a179d7af27be70a045c80.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[= \\frac{3n \\cdot \\log {2}}{\\log {2n}} \\cdot (1+ \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} + \\dots)\\]\" width=\"242\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/e/c1e9de64a8befac0ff1d312b7135cb5993bda7b2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[= \\frac{6n \\cdot \\log {2}}{\\log {2n}}\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a8683875b868b1ff1fc2fd3dc7a29ca20494c3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\frac{\\log{64} \\cdot n}{\\log{n}}\\]\" width=\"88\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>As we have previously derived,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/f/13f577fee75b34f38f32243705e6e782c99fa64c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\pi (2n) - \\pi (n) \\le \\log {4} \\cdot \\frac{n}{\\log {n}}\\]\" width=\"216\" height=\"36\" />\n</p><p>so\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/a/d1a97ebf74ee482d8ce03c91f7abc8e771266124.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\log {x}}{2^m} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{x}{2^m}) - \\frac{\\log {x}}{2^{m+1}} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}}) = \\frac{\\log {x}}{2^{m}} (\\pi (\\frac{x}{2^m})-\\pi (\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}})) + \\frac{\\log {x}}{2^{m+1}} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}})\\]\" width=\"564\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/f/71fea4f3712b06adfcaba11661c5d02609850e8e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\frac{\\log {x}}{2^{m}} \\cdot \\log {4} \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}}}{\\log {\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}}}} + \\frac{\\log {x}}{2^{m+1}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log{64} \\cdot \\frac{x}{2^{m+2}}}{\\log{\\frac{x}{2^{m+2}}}}\\]\" width=\"360\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/3/af3d799baebf30b011f8c86908ea1267ed42bc1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\frac{\\log {x} \\cdot \\log {4}}{2^{2m+1}} \\cdot (\\frac{x}{\\log {\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}}}}+\\frac{x}{\\log {\\frac{x}{2^{m+2}}}})\\]\" width=\"288\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/81618f798bcff20cf7a45fb0515ad598a4c5521a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\frac{\\log {x} \\cdot \\log {4}}{2^{2m}} \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\log {x}- (m+2) \\log {2}}\\]\" width=\"282\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/6/7464487efce949f4909dfeb824216100fe133244.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\log {4} \\cdot \\frac{x}{2^{m}}\\]\" width=\"90\" height=\"32\" />\n</p><p>. (The last step I do not know why. If you can figure out the reason, please add the intermediate steps for it)\n</p><p>If we add that, we get a telescoping sum, so we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/f/f1f51e257a9f415871f30fea2f7fd0caae40c7da.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\log{x} \\cdot \\pi (x)  = \\sum_{m=0}^{v} (\\frac{\\log {x}}{2^m} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{x}{2^m}) - \\frac{\\log {x}}{2^{m+1}} \\cdot \\pi (\\frac{x}{2^{m+1}}))\\]\" width=\"401\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/9/7a92867a82799f0e5cbb8bf5d5649abc7a32ebe7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\log{4} \\cdot x \\sum_{m=0}^{v} \\frac{1}{2^m}\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/a/7aabb86e5444bd4b8264de8271f2f1be1ae8a132.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\le \\log{4} \\cdot x\\]\" width=\"76\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/a/05ac5bd8cb213b130f9d6fc8302dde9d0d970043.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies \\pi (x) \\le \\log {4} \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\log{x}}\\]\" width=\"186\" height=\"36\" />\n</p><p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the greatest integer such a that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/c/d1cceb32d2017f5dac30350818ee15df65c389cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^v | x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Collecting our results, we see that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/5/4f52aff88deabcd7ae68ee4943a99258f9c74d2a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\log{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{n}{\\log {n}} \\le \\pi (x) \\le \\log {4} \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\log{x}}\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>We have thus found the desired <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00322295674c54c5413520f7493470ff32ae3cec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d4142d351ef99cc26a94e44c1912d904dd4a0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" />. ~Ddk001 <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Note: This was actually the same method of proof given by Chebyshev.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chebyshev_and_the_history_of_the_Prime_Number_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_Theory\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Number Theory\">Number Theory</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_Number_Theorem\" title=\"Prime Number Theorem\">Prime Number Theorem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_Theory_Problems_Collection&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Number Theory Problems Collection (page does not exist)\">Number Theory Problems Collection</a> (This is where the proof originated)</li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018080245\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.029 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.056 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 311/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 12517/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:30572:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018080245 and revision id 24836